1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:05,396
One tiny bulb fails on Aarav's old
string of festival lights, and the whole

2
00:00:05,396 --> 00:00:06,746
string goes dark.

3
00:00:07,096 --> 00:00:11,006
But when one bulb fails at home,
every other light stays on.

4
00:00:11,006 --> 00:00:11,906
Why?

5
00:00:11,996 --> 00:00:14,584
The answer is how the parts are connected.

6
00:00:14,584 --> 00:00:16,229
There are two basic ways.

7
00:00:16,229 --> 00:00:17,674
Series, and parallel.

8
00:00:18,024 --> 00:00:22,001
In series, the parts sit one
after another, in a single path.

9
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:23,697
Like beads on one thread.

10
00:00:24,047 --> 00:00:28,518
In parallel, each part gets its own
path, connected across the same two

11
00:00:28,518 --> 00:00:29,418
points.

12
00:00:29,162 --> 00:00:30,724
Like lanes on a road.

13
00:00:31,374 --> 00:00:33,673
Let's build both on the breadboard.

14
00:00:33,673 --> 00:00:38,171
A twelve volt supply, and two
resistors, four ohms and twelve ohms.

15
00:00:38,521 --> 00:00:39,905
First, in series.

16
00:00:39,905 --> 00:00:44,138
There is only one path, so the
same current flows through both.

17
00:00:44,138 --> 00:00:46,195
Zero point seven five amperes.

18
00:00:46,545 --> 00:00:48,411
But the voltage is shared.

19
00:00:48,411 --> 00:00:52,819
Three volts across the four ohm
resistor, nine volts across the twelve

20
00:00:52,819 --> 00:00:53,848
ohm resistor.

21
00:00:54,198 --> 00:00:55,876
Now break one resistor.

22
00:00:55,876 --> 00:00:57,514
The only path is cut.

23
00:00:57,514 --> 00:00:58,762
Everything stops.

24
00:00:58,762 --> 00:01:00,757
That is the festival string.

25
00:01:01,407 --> 00:01:03,415
Now rebuild them in parallel.

26
00:01:03,415 --> 00:01:06,969
Each resistor sits right
across the twelve volt supply.

27
00:01:07,319 --> 00:01:09,798
Three amperes take the four ohm path.

28
00:01:09,798 --> 00:01:12,277
One ampere takes the twelve ohm path.

29
00:01:12,277 --> 00:01:14,855
The supply gives four amperes in total.

30
00:01:15,205 --> 00:01:16,780
Break one resistor now.

31
00:01:16,780 --> 00:01:19,987
Its path stops, but the
other path keeps flowing.

32
00:01:19,987 --> 00:01:23,348
On the scope, the total
drops, but never to zero.

33
00:01:23,998 --> 00:01:25,601
Series shares the voltage.

34
00:01:25,601 --> 00:01:27,294
Parallel shares the current.

35
00:01:28,094 --> 00:01:31,589
Now let's find the total
resistance, starting with series.

36
00:01:31,939 --> 00:01:34,054
The supply voltage is shared.

37
00:01:34,054 --> 00:01:36,137
V equals V one plus V two.

38
00:01:36,837 --> 00:01:39,366
The current I is the same in both.

39
00:01:39,366 --> 00:01:41,969
So V equals IR one plus IR two.

40
00:01:42,669 --> 00:01:43,874
Divide by I.

41
00:01:43,874 --> 00:01:46,962
The series total is R one plus R two.

42
00:01:46,962 --> 00:01:48,810
Resistances simply add.

43
00:01:49,510 --> 00:01:50,550
Now parallel.

44
00:01:50,550 --> 00:01:53,496
The voltage V is the same across each one.

45
00:01:53,846 --> 00:01:55,181
The currents add.

46
00:01:55,181 --> 00:01:57,237
I equals I one plus I two.

47
00:01:57,937 --> 00:02:00,227
Each current is V over R.

48
00:02:00,227 --> 00:02:04,832
So V over R equals V over
R one, plus V over R two.

49
00:02:05,532 --> 00:02:06,689
Divide by V.

50
00:02:06,689 --> 00:02:10,993
One over R equals one over
R one, plus one over R two.

51
00:02:10,993 --> 00:02:14,970
The total is always smaller
than the smallest resistor.

52
00:02:15,620 --> 00:02:16,756
Worked example.

53
00:02:16,756 --> 00:02:21,197
A six ohm and a three ohm resistor,
with an eighteen volt supply.

54
00:02:21,547 --> 00:02:24,662
In series, R is six plus three, nine ohms.

55
00:02:24,662 --> 00:02:27,768
The current is eighteen
by nine, two amperes.

56
00:02:28,118 --> 00:02:33,276
In parallel, one over R is one sixth
plus one third, which is one half.

57
00:02:33,276 --> 00:02:34,750
So R is two ohms.

58
00:02:35,100 --> 00:02:38,351
The supply then gives
eighteen by two, nine amperes.

59
00:02:38,351 --> 00:02:42,896
Three through the six ohm resistor,
and six through the three ohm resistor.

60
00:02:43,546 --> 00:02:45,530
Homes are wired in parallel.

61
00:02:45,530 --> 00:02:50,658
Every appliance gets the full mains
voltage, and each one has its own switch.

62
00:02:51,308 --> 00:02:52,773
Now, the exam trap.

63
00:02:53,123 --> 00:02:56,027
Many students work out
one over R, and stop.

64
00:02:56,027 --> 00:02:57,625
They write half an ohm.

65
00:02:57,975 --> 00:02:59,919
Always flip it at the end.

66
00:02:59,919 --> 00:03:03,034
One over R is one half, so R is two ohms.

67
00:03:03,684 --> 00:03:05,300
Where is this asked?

68
00:03:05,300 --> 00:03:10,151
In PUC board papers, as problems on
combinations of resistors, and the

69
00:03:10,151 --> 00:03:12,918
differences between series and parallel.

70
00:03:13,568 --> 00:03:14,468
Try this.

71
00:03:14,383 --> 00:03:16,631
Three resistors of six ohms each.

72
00:03:16,631 --> 00:03:21,197
Two are joined in parallel, and this
pair is in series with the third,

73
00:03:21,197 --> 00:03:22,938
across a nine volt cell.

74
00:03:22,938 --> 00:03:26,559
What current flows through
one of the parallel resistors?

75
00:03:31,959 --> 00:03:32,859
A.

76
00:03:32,468 --> 00:03:34,567
Zero point five amperes.

77
00:03:34,917 --> 00:03:38,083
The parallel pair is
six by two, three ohms.

78
00:03:38,083 --> 00:03:39,763
Add the third resistor.

79
00:03:39,763 --> 00:03:41,238
Nine ohms in total.

80
00:03:41,588 --> 00:03:44,829
The current from the cell
is nine by nine, one ampere.

81
00:03:45,179 --> 00:03:48,366
It splits equally between
two equal resistors.

82
00:03:48,366 --> 00:03:51,509
So each one carries
zero point five amperes.

83
00:03:52,159 --> 00:03:53,681
Screenshot this card.

84
00:03:56,881 --> 00:03:59,622
Next, Kirchhoff's
current and voltage laws.

85
00:03:59,622 --> 00:04:02,870
Two rules that solve any
circuit, however tangled.
